# SOST V16.2 — official binary hashes
#
# Release artifact for tag v16.2.0 (commit 8f6b9ddf4df857d1cfb9ef8b6aeec566be93a14e).
# Committed AFTER that tag on purpose: a hash of a binary built from a tag cannot live
# inside that tag. The tag is not moved and never will be.
#
# These supersede the v16.1.0 hashes for verifying a CURRENT binary. The V16.1 list is
# kept alongside, as docs/v16/SHA256SUMS_V16_1, because it is what a rollback binary
# must verify against — do not delete it.
#
# V16.2.0 changes NO consensus rule. It is not a fork, it activates nothing, and a
# V16.1.0 node and a V16.2.0 node follow the same chain. The binaries still differ,
# because the code that handles passwords and keys changed, so a V16.2 binary MUST NOT
# verify against the V16.1 list — that is correct, not tampering.
#
# Verify:   sha256sum -c docs/v16/SHA256SUMS
#
# Built from the tag with the mainnet flags, in the environment recorded in
# docs/v16/RELEASE_MANIFEST_V16_2.md:
#   cmake -S . -B build -DSOST_ENABLE_PHASE2_SBPOW=ON -DSOST_TESTNET_FORKS=OFF \
#         -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
#   cmake --build build --target sost-node sost-miner sost-cli -j"$(nproc)"
#
# The build directory must be named `build`, inside the source tree. The source path
# itself does not matter (-ffile-prefix-map normalises it), and that is measured: the
# same commit built at /home/sost/SOST/sostcore/rel-v162-a and at /tmp/rel-v162-b
# produced these exact bytes. A build directory with a DIFFERENT NAME (build-v162,
# out/, …) produces different bytes from the same source — expected, not tampering.
#
# PATH-INDEPENDENT BUILD = YES   — measured again for this release, two directories.
# CROSS-MACHINE REPRODUCIBILITY = NOT PROVEN — a different compiler, linker or libc can
#                                  still produce different bytes. For an OFFICIAL
#                                  downloaded binary the hash must match exactly.
